


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: janutenaite-bogdaniene-kucinskas
@title: Multimodal Encoding of Čiurlionis’ Vocal Music through MEI–TEI Integration and Open LLM Evaluation
@presenter: Jūratė Janutėnaitė-Bogdanienė<sup>1</sup> &amp; Darius Kučinskas<sup>2</sup>
@affiliation: (1) Vilnius Gediminas Technical University, Vilnius, Lithuania (2) Kaunas University of Technology, Kaunas, Lithuania 
@date: 2024-10-24
@time: 12:00
@abstract: 

	Mikalojus Konstantinas Čiurlionis (1875–1911), a Lithuanian
	composer and painter, developed a body of work in which 
	musical and visual modes of thought are tightly intertwined.
	Although his piano compositions have recently been made
	openly accessible through an online platform providing
	manuscript facsimiles, critical editions, MEI encodings,
	and interactive analytical tools, comparable computational
	support for his vocal music remains lacking. Vocal works
	pose additional challenges due to the need to represent,
	align, and compare historically evolving textual content
	alongside musical structure. This paper presents an
	extension of an existing digital environment for the
	analysis of Čiurlionis’s music that brings his vocal works
	into focus. The project combines established music-encoding
	practices with methods from digital text scholarship to
	represent both music and lyrics in a flexible, historically
	sensitive way. Multiple versions of song texts are encoded
	and related directly to musical passages, allowing scholars
	to examine how changes in words and changes in music
	interact. In addition, the framework serves as a testing
	ground for the use of open large language models (LLMs) in
	musicological practice. We explore how such models can
	assist in transforming lyric transcriptions into structured
	scholarly formats and in relating textual variants to
	musical settings. Several open LLMs are compared with
	regard to their reliability, consistency, and suitability
	for historically complex poetic sources. The resulting data
	model integrates textual and musical materials while
	remaining compatible with existing encodings of
	instrumental works. On this basis, we develop tools for
	searching, comparing, and analysing the relationship
	between text and music across different versions of a work.
	By combining close attention to Čiurlionis’s vocal
	repertoire with an evaluation of emerging digital methods,
	this study offers a general framework for the digital study
	of vocal music and reflects on the role of AI-based tools
	in musicological research. The presented research is
	granted by Lithuanian Research Council (S-LISm-25-7).

@@END: PRESENTATION



